1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,445
Good evening, excellence.

2
00:00:01,445 --> 00:00:03,331
You have a written,

3
00:00:03,331 --> 00:00:05,217
signed procedure, filed away.

4
00:00:05,217 --> 00:00:06,344
But if you went to

5
00:00:06,344 --> 00:00:07,245
the floor right now…

6
00:00:07,245 --> 00:00:08,418
would you find anyone

7
00:00:08,418 --> 00:00:09,590
doing it as written?

8
00:00:09,617 --> 00:00:11,241
The auditor walked onto the

9
00:00:11,241 --> 00:00:12,540
line, procedure in hand.

10
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:14,760
The procedure says: seven steps.

11
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:16,982
The worker does only five,

12
00:00:16,982 --> 00:00:18,760
fast, with zero mistakes.

13
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:21,255
The auditor wrote: nonconformity.

14
00:00:21,255 --> 00:00:22,540
But the real question:

15
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:23,607
Who is wrong?

16
00:00:23,607 --> 00:00:24,321
The worker?

17
00:00:24,321 --> 00:00:25,293
Or the paper?

18
00:00:25,293 --> 00:00:26,693
Look at any garden:

19
00:00:26,693 --> 00:00:28,026
the paved walkway here,

20
00:00:28,026 --> 00:00:29,150
and a path people have

21
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:30,274
worn through the grass there.

22
00:00:30,274 --> 00:00:32,226
The procedure on paper is the walkway,

23
00:00:32,226 --> 00:00:33,255
and the real work

24
00:00:33,255 --> 00:00:34,283
is the worn path.

25
00:00:34,283 --> 00:00:36,331
The wider the gap between them,

26
00:00:36,331 --> 00:00:37,655
the more it becomes

27
00:00:37,655 --> 00:00:38,979
paper just for auditors.

28
00:00:38,979 --> 00:00:41,017
This procedure was written three years ago,

29
00:00:41,017 --> 00:00:43,074
and the machine changed last year.

30
00:00:43,074 --> 00:00:44,799
The worker found a faster way,

31
00:00:44,799 --> 00:00:46,236
and nobody updated the paper.

32
00:00:46,236 --> 00:00:48,207
The fix isn’t punishing the worker…

33
00:00:48,207 --> 00:00:50,607
it’s writing the procedure together with them.

34
00:00:50,607 --> 00:00:52,283
Try the 15-minute test:

35
00:00:52,283 --> 00:00:54,052
Print one procedure and stand next to

36
00:00:54,052 --> 00:00:55,569
whoever does it for 15 minutes.

37
00:00:55,569 --> 00:00:56,940
Mark each step:

38
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:58,893
Done? Done differently?

39
00:00:58,893 --> 00:01:00,712
Or not there at all?

40
00:01:00,712 --> 00:01:01,626
You’ll be surprised.

41
00:01:01,626 --> 00:01:04,750
And ISO 9001 requires exactly this.

42
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:06,102
Clause 7.5:

43
00:01:06,102 --> 00:01:08,207
Documented information must be

44
00:01:08,207 --> 00:01:10,312
suitable, with controlled updates.

45
00:01:10,312 --> 00:01:11,683
And clause 8.1:

46
00:01:11,683 --> 00:01:14,102
Processes are carried out as planned.

47
00:01:14,102 --> 00:01:15,692
So the paper and the floor

48
00:01:15,692 --> 00:01:17,017
must be the same thing.

49
00:01:17,017 --> 00:01:18,845
But if the procedure is followed correctly…

50
00:01:18,845 --> 00:01:20,448
how do you know it’s

51
00:01:20,448 --> 00:01:21,731
excellent, not just present?

52
00:01:21,731 --> 00:01:24,323
The EFQM Model has a five-letter

53
00:01:24,323 --> 00:01:26,483
word that reads both ways.

54
00:01:26,483 --> 00:01:27,550
What do you think it is?

55
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:28,760
Tell me in the comments.

56
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:31,004
Save this episode. See you

57
00:01:31,004 --> 00:01:33,248
next time on Masaa Al-Tamayoz.
